artifact.self_contained_problem:
A tensegrity framework consists of a finite set of rigid bars (struts) and tensile elements (cables) connected at nodes, parameterized by the nodal coordinates q ∈ R^N. A configuration q is called feasible if it satisfies: (i) bar-length constraints (each bar maintains its fixed length), (ii) cable constraints (each cable length does not exceed its natural length unless under tension), (iii) prestress equilibrium under the chosen actuation (rest-length) parameters, (iv) non-intersection of bars (no two bars share interior points), (v) any prescribed environmental constraints (e.g., obstacle avoidance, contact with a ground plane), and (vi) the kinematic and dynamic constraints of the underlying robotic actuation model (bounded actuator velocities, forces, and admissible cable tensions). The set F of all feasible configurations forms a subset of R^N that is generically embedded in a submanifold of dimension strictly smaller than N.

Given two feasible configurations q_0, q_1 ∈ F, develop a constructive algorithm that produces a continuous trajectory q : [0,1] → F with q(0) = q_0 and q(1) = q_1, together with a corresponding actuation schedule (rest-length variations of active members) compatible with the robot's kinematic and dynamic model along the entire trajectory. The algorithm should: 
(a) guarantee that every intermediate configuration q(t) lies in F (in particular, satisfies the prestress equilibrium and non-bar-intersection conditions);
(b) respect actuator bounds and dynamic feasibility;
(c) handle the lower-dimensional embedding of F intrinsically rather than by relying on generic ambient-space search.

Provide either a general method that solves this problem for arbitrary tensegrity topologies, or characterize the classes of tensegrities for which such an algorithm is guaranteed to succeed.

